\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{Cospectral graphs with the same selector}
\date{2026-11-01}

\p{There are [selected backgrounds](fdip-005A) related by the
[orthogonal comparison](fdip-005D) with no [signed graph arrow](fdip-005C).
On vertices #{\{0,1,2,3,4,5\}}, take simple graphs with
unit weights and the following unordered edges:
##{\begin{aligned}
E_A&=\{02,03,04,05,14,15,23\},\\
E_B&=\{02,04,05,12,14,15,23\}.
\end{aligned}}
Orient each edge from its lower to its higher endpoint, choose #{m=1},
and in both cases choose #{P=\mathbf1\mathbf1^T/6}, the projector
onto the constant line. Both graphs are connected. Their degree
multisets are #{(4,2,2,2,2,2)} and #{(3,3,3,2,2,1)}, so even
their unoriented incidence presentations are not isomorphic.}

\proof{
\p{For graph #{A}, the differences on the twin pairs #{\{2,3\}}
and #{\{4,5\}} have Laplacian eigenvalues #{3} and #{2}.
On fields constant on the cells #{\{0\}}, #{\{1\}}, #{\{2,3\}}, #{\{4,5\}}, in that order,
the remaining matrix is
##{M_A=\begin{pmatrix}
4&0&-2&-2\\0&2&0&-2\\-1&0&1&0\\-1&-1&0&2
\end{pmatrix}.}
For graph #{B}, use twin differences on #{\{0,1\}} and #{\{4,5\}},
again of eigenvalues #{3} and #{2}. On cells
#{\{0,1\}}, #{\{2\}}, #{\{3\}}, #{\{4,5\}}, in that order, the matrix is
##{M_B=\begin{pmatrix}
3&-1&0&-2\\-2&3&-1&0\\0&-1&1&0\\-2&0&0&2
\end{pmatrix}.}
These are cell-value coordinates, so the displayed quotient matrices
need not be symmetric. In each case expansion gives
##{\det(tI-M_A)=\det(tI-M_B)=t(t-3)(t^2-6t+4).}
Together with the two difference eigenvectors, this proves the common
Laplacian spectrum #{0,2,3,3,3-\sqrt5,3+\sqrt5}.
Choose orthonormal eigenbases with the same normalized constant vector
#{\mathbf1 / \sqrt6} first, and map one basis to the other.
The resulting #{O} conjugates #{L_A} to #{L_B}, hence also
#{Q_A=L_A+I} to #{Q_B=L_B+I}. It fixes the constant line and
therefore transports #{P} as well. This proves the selected comparison.
The degree multisets exclude every signed graph arrow.}
}
